# Input
```js
/* should generate diagnostics */
import { defineComponent, watch, watchEffect } from "vue";

defineComponent(
	async (props, context) => {
		await doSomething();
		watch(source, () => {});
	},
	{ props: ["source"] },
);

defineComponent(
	async function (props) {
		await doSomething();
		watchEffect(() => {});
	},
	{ props: ["source"] },
);

```

# Diagnostics
```
invalidDefineComponentSetupFunction.js:7:3 lint/nursery/noVueWatchAfterAwait ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

  × This watcher is created after an await, so it keeps running after the component is removed.
  
    5 │ 	async (props, context) => {
    6 │ 		await doSomething();
  > 7 │ 		watch(source, () => {});
      │ 		^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    8 │ 	},
    9 │ 	{ props: ["source"] },
  
  i The setup() function pauses here. After this point, Vue no longer knows which component the watcher belongs to.
  
    4 │ defineComponent(
    5 │ 	async (props, context) => {
  > 6 │ 		await doSomething();
      │ 		^^^^^
    7 │ 		watch(source, () => {});
    8 │ 	},
  
  i Create the watcher before the first await in setup(), or store the function it returns and call that function to stop the watcher.
  
  i This rule belongs to the nursery group, which means it is not yet stable and may change in the future. Visit https://biomejs.dev/linter/#nursery for more information.
  

```

